Rebased onto the latest `main` and resolved the conflicts — the metrics-reporter test fixtures were refactored upstream in the meantime, so the tests here are adapted to the new constructor shape. Both CPU unit files pass locally (16 and 5 tests).

@merrymercy @fzyzcjy @hnyls2002 — this extends the per-iteration forward-pass metrics introduced in #22789 with decode-interference visibility: how much non-decode work is landing inside decode steps and stretching inter-token latency. When decode slows down, the existing metrics tell you *that* it slowed, but not whether prefill, grammar work, or another scheduler-side cost displaced it — which is the first question you ask during an incident and currently the one you can't answer without attaching a profiler to a production process.

**On the red checks:** they're `pr-gate` / `pr-test-*-finish` failing for a missing `run-ci` label — nothing has actually run. I can't add the label (not in `.github/CI_PERMISSIONS.json`); could a maintainer add it?

@RichardoMrMu @hnyls2002 could you confirm the two earlier review concerns are addressed on `572249ea21b7`, and authorize `run-ci` for GPU/overlap validation?

Forward timing now claims each batch's own device-timer segments; the scheduler-to-result interval is exported separately. Both exporters use the canonical final-PP/attention-TP0/CP0 rank, with DP series kept separate. The two review threads have implementation/test replies and remain open for your confirmation.

Recorded local validation on this head is 58 tests and 51 subtests on CPU with fakes/stubs. Actual GPU timing, overlap execution and performance remain unverified; the current CI gate stops at the missing `run-ci` label. Once those checks run, please let me know if any other design or validation requirement remains before this can merge.

